00:00:04.789 --> 00:00:06.230 align:start position:0%
adrian cambana should be looking forward  to christmas  but instead this year  like the three before  she'll be without her husband  jimmy mubenga died as he was being  deported to angola  he was on a plane  on a runway at heathrow airport  but just before takeoff  something went tragically wrong  i was in my home when the police called  me but i didn't know it was there i  thought it was jimmy  because they used jimmy's phone  jimmy mubinga died on board the aircraft  after being heavily restrained for more  than half an hour in a position that  would eventually kill him  terence hughes seen here on your right  colin kayla  and stuart treibelnig were the custody  officers who were tasked with taking mr  mubinga back to africa  his death  led to them facing a charge of  manslaughter  several witnesses on board flight ba77  say they heard jimmy mubenga pleading  for his life  let me go i can't breathe he said over  and over again  the prosecution claims the defendants  ignored those pleas  they say they didn't hear those words  uttered  whenever we're taking an escort  it's important to make sure that in the  seated position the escort is kept as  upright as possible eric baskind is an  expert on the use of force  he showed us how mr mubenga should have  been restrained so from this relaxed  position in the event that the detainee  starts to struggle  then the guard behind can make sure that  his head is kept upright  that the staff either side can restrain  him and in the event that he's  struggling he's going to be kept in an  upright position  and then he showed us how mr mubinga was  held  so in the event of a struggle what would  happen is that the guard in front was  pulling the head down and pulling the  head down in any of these positions  that's almost certainly the most  dangerous position that we can think of  in restraint it's simply because their  lung function is going to be compromised  they will have difficulty in breathing  mr mubinga's death has led to a review  in the way forced deportations are  carried out by the government  but campaigners say the process is still  not transparent enough  an earlier inquest into jimmy benga's  death led the cps to re-examine the case  and criminal charges and the trial  followed  adrienne has welcomed the change in the  way deportees are restrained  but it's come too late for her i don't  want to see any another family to suffer  like  we have done  it's very very very painful  ashish joshi sky news
 

